<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iazyges</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jazyges</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Iazyges were a Sarmatian people who settled on the Great Hungarian Plain between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a> in the early first century CE. They fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcomannic-wars">Marcomannic Wars</a>; after their defeat in 175 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> sent 5,500 of their horsemen to serve in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. They remained Roman neighbours and clients until the arrival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Iazyges were a Sarmatian people who travelled west from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> to the steppes of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. They are generally identified with the earlier Iaxamatae, recorded around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-azov">Sea of Azov</a> from about 500 BCE. Ancient writers disagreed about how to classify that people: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephorus">Ephorus</a> counted the Iaxamatae among the Sauromatae, while Demetrius of Callatis held them to be Maeotian. Modern scholars treat &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeotians">Maeotians</a>&quot; as a geographical term for the peoples around the Sea of Azov and agree that the Iaxamatae were Iranian nomads. They later settled between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a> on the Pannonian plain, where they became semi-sedentary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> the Iazyges first served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as a buffer against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacians">Dacians</a>, and the relationship later became that of overlord and client. In both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajans-dacian-wars">Dacian Wars</a> they fought on the Roman side, according to the account, helping to stop Dacian incursions and then defeating the Dacians. A dispute over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oltenia">Oltenia</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> took during the Second Dacian War, led to a war in 107 and 108, in which Hadrian, then governor of Pannonia Inferior, defeated them. They raided Roman land throughout, which brought punitive expeditions.</p><p>In 169 the Iazyges, Quadi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a> and Marcomanni invaded Roman territory again. The Iazyges attacked Alburnum to seize its gold mines, and Marcus Claudius Fronto held them back until he was killed in 170</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> defeated them, and the peace imposed restrictions: they could not settle on the islands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> or keep boats on it, but they were allowed to trade with the Roxolani through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a> and in Roman markets on set days. From the 4th century Roman writers stopped distinguishing among the Sarmatian tribes. In the 5th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> conquered the two Sarmatian peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a>, and by the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> they were absorbed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Iazyges kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, moved between their towns to graze them, and received grain from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a>. Their capital was at Partiscum, roughly on the site of Kecskemét in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and records name eight towns.</p><p>Their army was exclusively cavalry: riders in scale armour wore sugarloaf helmets and wielded long two-handed lances called the contus from armoured horses. Graves were flat, rectangular or circular, and grouped like modern cemeteries; later tombs are far richer, holding weapons in 86 percent of the burials of the 2nd to early 4th centuries. Their language was a dialect of Old Iranian that differed from most other Sarmatian speech. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> never made their territory a province, and the land, largely swamp without mineable metals, may explain why.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iazyges” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
